From 08dba620ebd225b27df95f377f982d88923c00a2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 19:59:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(gateway): pending notices ride the turn that starts at a
 boundary [daveey]

A background-process completion admitted mid-turn waited one full turn per row queued
ahead of it. On 2026-11-13 proc_467899b1fa1b's completion was admitted at 10:21:51 in
MacBook chat 20261026_235722_bef6c6 and reached the model at 11:21:07 (59 min). Three
update-resume continuations and one Kanban batch each started a turn without it
(t_01a1d663082aa53a).

Five whys:
1. Completions are delivered at turn boundaries, and the chat was mid-turn.
2. Four boundaries in a row each started one turn from another queued row, and none of
   those turns carried the pending completion.
3. The drain claims exactly one row per turn. The admission-time folds
   (_admit_folded_completion, kanban_wake_fold) only join a row of the same kind at the
   queue tail. An update-resume head is a started checkpoint, not a queued row, so
   nothing could fold into it.
Root (happened): turn composition never looked past the claimed row.
Root (not caught): no test had two different kinds of notice queued at one boundary,
or a notice pending behind an update-resume.

Fix: gateway/session_fold_pending.py. When an update-resume continuation, a completion
turn or a Kanban-wake turn starts (execute_admission, after the pause note is built),
every completion and Kanban wake still queued for the session joins it. The scan stops
at the first human input and skips held notifier wakes, liveness/loop ticks and remote
notices that carry kanban_event_keys. Rows join in admission order, and each keeps its
own text (Kanban rows are refreshed from live card state first). A person's message,
and finite/API/CLI turns, are never extended.

Custody reuses the queued-steer transfer. Each row goes queued -> terminal 'steering',
bound to the claim's generation, and settles in that claim's own settlement write.
settle_outcome marks a row delivered only when the claim completed, so it is acked
exactly once as 'steered'. Any other ending (failed, interrupted, unknown, an
unstarted requeue) puts it back in its own FIFO place. A turn parking for a Hermes
update first reverts its folded rows (unfold_for_checkpoint), because a steering
transfer refuses the checkpoint; the continuation folds them again. The queue's own
fold rules and stored rows are unchanged.

Proof (tests/gateway/test_fold_pending_admissions.py), RED on origin/main (5 of 6 fail)
and GREEN here:
a. a completion admitted while a Kanban turn is paused for an update rides the resume
   turn: pause_note(paused request) + completion.
b. Kanban row then completion behind a busy turn: one turn, in admission order.
c. two completions plus one Kanban row: one turn, with the head completed and the
   others steered. A failed turn requeues both folded rows and the next turn carries
   them. A paused turn requeues them and the resume turn carries them. No row is left
   pending.
Guard: a queued prompt is never jumped or extended.
The 177 test files that import the touched modules (kanban_wake_fold, completion_fold,
process registry, notifications, update pause, steer, local producer admission) pass:
1500 passed, 13 skipped. scripts/check --base origin/main is ok.
